# Git Workflow

## Branch Naming
- `feature/<short-desc>` - new feature
- `fix/<short-desc>` - bug fix
- `chore/<short-desc>` - maintenance, deps
- `docs/<short-desc>` - documentation
- `refactor/<short-desc>` - refactoring
- `hotfix/<short-desc>` - urgent production fix

Example: `feature/auth-jwt`, `fix/login-validation`

## Conventional Commits
Format: `<type>(<scope>): <description>`

**Types:**
- `feat` - new feature
- `fix` - bug fix
- `chore` - maintenance, deps, config
- `docs` - documentation only
- `style` - formatting, missing semicolons
- `refactor` - code change that neither fixes bug nor adds feature
- `perf` - performance improvement
- `test` - add or fix tests
- `build` - build system, CI

**Examples:**
```
feat(auth): add JWT refresh token rotation
fix(posts): resolve null reference in posts query
chore(deps): upgrade prisma to 6.1.0
docs(readme): update setup instructions
```

**Rules:**
- Subject ≤ 72 chars
- Use imperative mood: "add" not "added"
- No period at end
- Body explains **why**, not what
- Reference issues: `Closes #123`

## Commit Workflow
1. Stage specific files (không `git add .`)
2. Write meaningful commit message
3. Verify: `git status` clean before push
4. Push to feature branch, NEVER to main

## PR Process
- Title: same as commit subject
- Description: **What** + **Why** + **How to test**
- Link related issues
- Add screenshots/videos for UI changes
- Request review from at least 1 person
- All CI checks must pass
- **NEVER** force push to main
